{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target conflates two distinct criteria into 'redundant probes of the same latent quality.' The verdict-shape's frontier-claim-honest criterion tests whether a candidate truthfully represents its own frontier status (an epistemic-integrity constraint), while the frontier-above-floor criterion tests whether the candidate exceeds a minimum threshold (a quality-minimum constraint). These are logically independent: a candidate can be honest about a low frontier (passing honesty, failing floor) or dishonest about a high frontier (passing floor, failing honesty). Calling them 'redundant' misrepresents the §11.3 structure, so the flag-sustained rationale is built on a false equivalence rather than a genuine assessment of the adjudication-ritual's criteria.","counterexample":[{"criterion_failed":"frontier-above-floor","criterion_passed":"frontier-claim-honest","scenario":"Candidate X honestly reports its frontier at level 0.2, below the floor of 0.5. It passes the honesty criterion (no misrepresentation) but fails the above-floor criterion."},{"criterion_failed":"frontier-claim-honest","criterion_passed":"frontier-above-floor","scenario":"Candidate Y claims a frontier at level 0.9 (above the 0.5 floor) but actually operates at 0.3. It passes the above-floor criterion (its claimed frontier exceeds floor) but fails the honesty criterion (claim is dishonest)."}],"premise":"The §11.3 verdict-shape mandates two logically independent criteria: (1) every surviving candidate must satisfy a frontier-claim-honest criterion, and (2) every surviving candidate must satisfy a frontier-above-floor criterion.","target":"11fd8636afb53e902107d68d331fe996019bea1967ae986dc01127dd30cd8e35"}]}